border. The colour‑1 bars disappear: they become background, or they get covered by the copies.\n\n**Checking against the demonstrations:**\n- **Pair 1.** The square at rows 8–11, cols 8–11 has bars above it, to its left and to its right.\n  - Upward: copies at rows 4–7 and 0–3.\n  - Left: copies at cols 4–7 and 0–3.\n  - Right: copies at cols 12–15, then 16–19 clipped to 16–18. That is why row 9 ends in `8 0 0`.\n  - The second square (rows 14–17) has a bar at the bottom and gets one copy at rows 18–21.\n- **Pair 3.** Repeating upward from rows 5–8 gives copies at rows 1–4 and then rows −3..0. Only the copy's last row survives at row 0, which explains why rows 0 and 1 are both `3333`. Repeating left gives cols 2–5 and −2..1. Its clipped columns are the square's columns 2 and 3, producing `0 3 3 0` in rows 6–7. This matches the output.\n- **Pair 2.** The rule fits the same way: one square tiled right and down.\n\n**Test.** There are two 4‑cell squares of colour 4.\n- Square A is at rows 6–9, cols 6–9. It has bars at the top, bottom and left, so it is tiled up, down and left.\n- Square B is at rows 12–15, cols 13–16. It has bars at the top and bottom, so it is tiled up and down.\n\nOverlapping copies have the same colour, so they don't conflict.\n\n**Construction stages.** First the canvas is made with the marker bars erased. Then each (square, direction) tiling is emitted as its own stage.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    objs = objects(g, bg=bg)\n    squares = [o for o in objs if o[\"box\"][2] > o[\"box\"][0] and o[\"box\"][3] > o[\"box\"][1]]\n    markers = [o for o in objs if not (o[\"box\"][2] > o[\"box\"][0] and o[\"box\"][3] > o[\"box\"][1])]\n    out = g.copy()\n    for m in markers:\n        for (y, x) in m[\"cells\"]:\n            out[y, x] = bg\n    emit(out, \"Erase the border marker bars; they only indicate directions.\")\n    for m in markers:\n        my0, mx0, my1, mx1 = m[\"box\"]\n        for s in squares:\n            y0, x0, y1, x1 = s[\"box\"]\n            h, w = y1 - y0 + 1, x1 - x0 + 1\n            if (mx0, mx1) == (x0, x1):\n                dy, dx = (-h, 0) if my0 < y0 else (h, 0)\n            elif (my0, my1) == (y0, y1):\n                dy, dx = (0, -w) if mx0 < x0 else (0, w)\n            else:\n                continue\n            patch = g[y0:y1 + 1, x0:x1 + 1]\n            k = 1\n            while True:\n                oy, ox = y0 + dy * k, x0 + dx * k\n                if oy > H - 1 or ox > W - 1 or oy + h - 1 < 0 or ox + w - 1 < 0:\n                    break\n                for i in range(h):\n                    for j in range(w):\n                        yy, xx = oy + i, ox + j\n                        if 0 <= yy < H and 0 <= xx < W:\n                            out[yy, xx] = patch[i, j]\n                k += 1\n            dname = {(-1, 0): \"up\", (1, 0): \"down\", (0, -1): \"left\", (0, 1): \"right\"}[(int(np.sign(dy)), int(np.sign(dx)))]\n            emit(out, f\"Tile the square at rows {y0}-{y1}, cols {x0}-{x1} {dname} toward its marker until the edge.\")\n    emit(out, \"Final grid.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"legend":[{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Erase the border marker bars; they only indicate directions."}],"repeat":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Pair 2.** The rule fits the same way: one square tiled right and down."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"It has bars at the top, bottom and left, so it is tiled up, down and left."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"It has bars at the top and bottom, so it is tiled up and down."}],"assembly":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Then each (square, direction) tiling is emitted as its own stage."}],"layers":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Overlapping copies have the same colour, so they don't conflict."}],"instructions":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**What the output does.** Each bar works as an instruction: \"repeat this square toward me.\" The square is copied over and over in steps of its own size (4 cells) in the bar's direction until the copies run off the grid."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"0c1b6088aaef70998e4afd7f5ae561f6fd7f2c97a2c9fb37a299ff940ef5374d","task_sha256":"f641ee714a7ad12a436336b36a2c9ee03d8c254030b9409d72a22ae84e7b3a17","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
